title: ADR-260 #7 — the thief's death raises the ceiling without moving the rank
story: dungeo
description: |
  Dungeo raises maxScore from 616 to 650 when the thief dies (ADR-078's hidden
  points, melee-interceptor.ts). Rank thresholds are ABSOLUTE POINTS, so that
  call must leave the player's rank exactly where it was. Under percentage
  bands it would silently DEMOTE a player who earned nothing and lost nothing —
  which is the whole reason ADR-260 D2 made thresholds absolute.

  Builds its own state via GDT — AA SCORE to reach a scored position, KL to
  kill the thief — so the rank is read on BOTH sides of the ceiling change.
  Never $restore: that made this a unit test wearing a chain test's clothes,
  dependent on walkthrough output that does not exist in a fresh checkout.
---

# ============================================================================
# GOAL: the ceiling moved; the rank did not
# ============================================================================

[GOAL: Rank is unchanged across the 616 -> 650 ceiling change]

# Position the score without replaying a walkthrough. 493 is the discriminating
# value: 493/616 = 80% against 493/650 = 76%, so a percentage ladder would have
# to re-rank here while an absolute threshold cannot.
> gdt
[OK: contains "GDT"]
> aa score 493
[OK: contains "493"]
> ex
[OK: contains "Returning"]

# BEFORE the ceiling change. The ledger is the single home for scoring
# (ADR-260 D1): SCORE reads it and nothing else. "Adventurer" is dungeo's own
# rank name — ported verbatim from ADR-085 — and appears in no platform source,
# which is what proves stdlib invented nothing (ADR-260 acceptance #3).
> score
[OK: contains "493 out of 616"]
[OK: contains "rank of an Adventurer"]

# Kill the thief. ADR-078: this raises maxScore to 650 and awards NO points
# ("No bonus points for killing the thief" — melee-interceptor.ts), so the
# score is held fixed while the ceiling alone moves.
> gdt
[OK: contains "GDT"]
> kl thief
[OK: contains "Killed"]
> ex
[OK: contains "Returning"]

# AFTER the ceiling change: same score, higher ceiling, SAME RANK.
# 400 <= 493 < 616 is "Adventurer" under the absolute threshold, and it stays
# "Adventurer" whether the ceiling reads 616 or 650.
> score
[OK: contains "493 out of 650"]
[OK: contains "rank of an Adventurer"]

# The five percentage-band ranks stdlib used to hardcode are gone (ADR-260 D4).
# If any of these appears, the deleted computeRank came back.
[OK: not contains "Novice"]
[OK: not contains "Proficient"]
[OK: not contains "Expert"]
[OK: not contains "Master"]

# Twice in a row: the rank is DERIVED from the ledger on every read, never
# stored, so two consecutive reads of an unchanged score agree exactly.
> score
[OK: contains "493 out of 650"]
[OK: contains "rank of an Adventurer"]

[END GOAL]
